A real NSLayoutConstraint, in the framework that already had a stub of it.

MONEYMONEY DIES ON +constraintWithItem: THE MOMENT APPKIT CLAIMS TO BE NEWER THAN 10.15, which is the
band that also changes its menu metric, so the two are tied together: the cosmetic fix in task #150
cannot land while the class is a stub.

The class belongs to AppKit on macOS and that is where the implementation was written first. It could
not stay there. THIS Foundation has carried an NSLayoutConstraint since the nib decoder needed
something to decode into, along with three subclasses of it, and two Objective-C classes with one
name is a coin toss the runtime announces and then resolves however it likes. It resolved to the
stub, whose methodSignatureForSelector answers "v@:" for everything, and +constraintWithItem: stayed
unrecognized however carefully AppKit implemented it. So the object lives here and AppKit imports
this header.

WHAT IT IS: the constraint, its properties, the factory, activate and deactivate, and -setActive:,
which puts the constraint on a view and takes it off again. Asked BY SELECTOR, because NSView is
AppKit's and this is Foundation.

WHAT IT IS NOT: a solver. Nothing here lays anything out. Every view in these nibs still carries an
autoresizing mask and translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints defaults to YES, so the layout is
exactly what it was. An application that genuinely lays out BY constraint gets a window whose
subviews sit where the mask puts them, which is wrong but visible, rather than an unrecognized
selector, which stops it dead.

MEASURED: with this and +[NSScreen screensHaveSeparateSpaces], MoneyMoney runs at
NSAppKitVersionNumber 2022 with zero unrecognized selectors and zero uncaught exceptions, and its
truncated menu titles gain the two characters the 35 point metric buys. The version number is still
1504 and the reason is in the capture, not the log: at 2022 the application draws no menu bar at all
and an empty toolbar, because it takes the Big Sur chrome path. See AppKit/NSApplication.m.
--- a/include/Foundation/NSLayoutConstraint.h
+++ b/include/Foundation/NSLayoutConstraint.h
@@ -1,5 +1,89 @@
 #import <Foundation/NSObject.h>
+#import <Foundation/NSArray.h>
+#import <Foundation/NSString.h>
 
-@interface NSLayoutConstraint : NSObject
+/*
+ * THE CLASS LIVES HERE BECAUSE IT ALREADY DID, and two of them is one too many.
+ *
+ * On macOS NSLayoutConstraint is AppKit's, and that is where the implementation was written first.
+ * It could not stay there: this Foundation has carried a stub of the same name since the nib
+ * decoder needed something to decode into, along with three subclasses of it, and a duplicate
+ * Objective-C class is a coin toss the runtime announces and then resolves however it likes. The
+ * application got the stub, whose methodSignatureForSelector answers "v@:" for everything, and
+ * +constraintWithItem: was unrecognized however carefully AppKit implemented it.
+ *
+ * So the object and its accounting live here, and AppKit imports this header. Nothing solves the
+ * constraints; see the note in AppKit/NSLayoutConstraint.h for why that is the right amount of Auto
+ * Layout for the applications in front of us.
+ */
+
+typedef float NSLayoutPriorityFoundation;
+
+typedef NS_ENUM(NSInteger, NSLayoutRelation) {
+    NSLayoutRelationLessThanOrEqual = -1,
+    NSLayoutRelationEqual = 0,
+    NSLayoutRelationGreaterThanOrEqual = 1,
+};
+
+typedef NS_ENUM(NSInteger, NSLayoutAttribute) {
+    NSLayoutAttributeNotAnAttribute = 0,
+    NSLayoutAttributeLeft = 1,
+    NSLayoutAttributeRight,
+    NSLayoutAttributeTop,
+    NSLayoutAttributeBottom,
+    NSLayoutAttributeLeading,
+    NSLayoutAttributeTrailing,
+    NSLayoutAttributeWidth,
+    NSLayoutAttributeHeight,
+    NSLayoutAttributeCenterX,
+    NSLayoutAttributeCenterY,
+    NSLayoutAttributeLastBaseline,
+    NSLayoutAttributeFirstBaseline,
+};
+
+enum {
+    NSLayoutPriorityRequired = 1000,
+    NSLayoutPriorityDefaultHigh = 750,
+    NSLayoutPriorityDragThatCanResizeWindow = 510,
+    NSLayoutPriorityWindowSizeStayPut = 500,
+    NSLayoutPriorityDragThatCannotResizeWindow = 490,
+    NSLayoutPriorityDefaultLow = 250,
+    NSLayoutPriorityFittingSizeCompression = 50,
+};
+
+@interface NSLayoutConstraint : NSObject {
+    id _firstItem;
+    id _secondItem;
+    NSLayoutAttribute _firstAttribute;
+    NSLayoutAttribute _secondAttribute;
+    NSLayoutRelation _relation;
+    double _multiplier;
+    double _constant;
+    float _priority;
+    BOOL _active;
+    NSString *_identifier;
+}
+
++ (instancetype) constraintWithItem: (id) firstItem
+                          attribute: (NSLayoutAttribute) firstAttribute
+                          relatedBy: (NSLayoutRelation) relation
+                             toItem: (id) secondItem
+                          attribute: (NSLayoutAttribute) secondAttribute
+                         multiplier: (double) multiplier
+                           constant: (double) constant;
+
++ (void) activateConstraints: (NSArray *) constraints;
++ (void) deactivateConstraints: (NSArray *) constraints;
+
+@property (readonly, assign) id firstItem;
+@property (readonly, assign) id secondItem;
+@property (readonly) NSLayoutAttribute firstAttribute;
+@property (readonly) NSLayoutAttribute secondAttribute;
+@property (readonly) NSLayoutRelation relation;
+@property (readonly) double multiplier;
+@property double constant;
+@property float priority;
+@property (getter=isActive) BOOL active;
+@property (copy) NSString *identifier;
 
 @end
